#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""fetch-scorecard: download the two College Scorecard CSVs bin/ingest-colleges takes as positionals.

RFC 173. Discovers the current release on collegescorecard.ed.gov, downloads the
institution-level and field-of-study archives of THAT release, extracts the one
data member from each, and writes the members plus a manifest:

    db/seed/scorecard/Most-Recent-Cohorts-Institution.csv     institution level
    db/seed/scorecard/Most-Recent-Cohorts-Field-of-Study.csv  field of study
    db/seed/scorecard/PROVENANCE.json  release, url, fetched_at, bytes, sha256
                                       per archive, plus the member taken, its
                                       digest, its data-row count and the
                                       bin/load-external-data POSITION it is
                                       passed as

The pair is fetched TOGETHER, from ONE release. bin/ingest-colleges cross-reads
them by UNITID, so an institution file from one release beside a field-of-study
file from another is a silent mismatch no header assertion can see.

The release stamp is DISCOVERED, not pinned, and that is this script's one
departure from bin/fetch-ipeds (which pins SURVEY_YEAR and its archive
names as literals). The two publishers publish differently: IPEDS names a survey
year that stays fetchable, so a year bump there is a reviewed edit of the script,
while the Scorecard publishes exactly one "most recent cohorts" release whose
predecessor's stamped URL goes away. Pinning the stamp would mean this script
starts 404ing on the publisher's schedule.

THE UNVERSIONED URLs ARE A TRAP AND ARE NEVER CONSTRUCTED HERE. The same two
names under /downloads/ with the _NNNNNNNN stamp LEFT OFF -- the strings this
file deliberately never spells in full, so that a grep for one finds nothing --
still return HTTP 200 and are Last-Modified 2024-08-22. A fetcher
pointed at it would ingest the August-2024 release forever, with a green exit and
a passing header assertion -- exactly the failure this repo's fetchers exist to
refuse. /downloads/ is itself a 404, so there is no directory listing: the stamp
is read off the /data/ page, and the URL that is downloaded is the href that page
served, never a string this script assembled.

So: GET https://collegescorecard.ed.gov/data/, match the hrefs, require EXACTLY
ONE match per artifact and require the two stamps to be EQUAL. Zero matches,
several matches for one artifact, or two disagreeing stamps are FATAL -- that is
a publisher change a human looks at, the same stance bin/fetch-ipeds takes on a
member it cannot resolve. A run whose discovered stamp differs from the one
PROVENANCE.json records LOGS the change and PROCEEDS: a new release is the whole
point of the fetcher, so it is reported, never refused. The member-drift guard
below is the one that refuses.

The members are NOT committed: extracted they are ~100 MB and ~153 MB against a
16 MB .git -- worse than the IPEDS members bin/fetch-ipeds already keeps out --
so db/seed/scorecard/ is gitignored except for PROVENANCE.json and
FETCH-NOTES.md. That manifest is the committed record of the real fetch AND the
baseline the shrink guard below reads, which is why an offline (-F) replay is
refused outright against a directory holding one that is not itself a fixture
replay.

The `fetched_at` CONTRACT, at both levels of the manifest (top level and per
artifact), is part of that record:

  * a network run writes ISO-8601 UTC to SECONDS precision, e.g.
    "2026-09-05T09:14:02+00:00";
  * a fixture (-F) replay writes null, and null means exactly that -- an offline
    replay -- so it is never a stand-in for "unknown";
  * `release` is null on a replay for the same reason: a recorded stamp would
    claim a published release the replay never read.

ALL member interpretation lives here (the ingest is handed plain CSV paths):

  * The member is resolved by EXACT name, after the __MACOSX/ directory entries
    and their AppleDouble ._* members are dropped. Most-Recent-Cohorts-Institution
    ships one of those beside the CSV, and it is precisely the wrong-member
    hazard: an archive missing its expected member is FATAL and the lone
    surviving member is never taken as a substitute.
  * Members are decoded utf-8-sig and re-encoded UTF-8, so no BOM survives into
    the UNITID header name the loaders match on.
  * Each artifact declares a SMALL set of IDENTITY columns -- UNITID, INSTNM,
    CONTROL, PREDDEG for the institution file; UNITID, CIPCODE, CREDLEV for the
    field of study -- and a header missing any of them is fatal. Each set holds
    at least one column the OTHER file does not carry (PREDDEG one way, CIPCODE
    and CREDLEV the other), which is what lets the check see a swap in either
    direction: UNITID, INSTNM and CONTROL alone appear in BOTH published
    headers. This is deliberately NOT
    a copy of the loaders' REQUIRED_INSTITUTION_COLUMNS: that list is the
    INGEST's contract, asserted there before any write (RFC 139), and a second
    copy here would drift the day a column is added to the Kotlin list. This
    check answers a different question -- did the extraction produce the
    institution file or the field-of-study one -- which is a fact about the
    FETCH. No column COUNT is asserted: 3,308 and 178 are today's numbers and
    the publisher adds columns.

Stdlib-only (dev-shell python3), no dependencies. Usage:

    fetch-scorecard [-o <outdir>] [-F <fixtures-dir>]

    -o DIR    extracted CSV + PROVENANCE.json directory
              (default: db/seed/scorecard under the repo root)
    -F DIR    offline mode for tests: read the recorded /data/ page and the
              recorded archives or CSVs from <dir> instead of downloading.
              REQUIRES an explicit -o: the recorded fixtures are a handful of
              rows while the committed manifest under the DEFAULT -o records the
              real release, so a replay into the default directory would either
              be refused or, worse, overwrite the committed record.
    -h        help

There is no -K: nothing here is authenticated. The bulk zips are anonymous
downloads. (The api.data.gov Scorecard API does need a key, and would need 63
paged requests for what one zip gives, so this script does not use it.)

Options are parsed with the stdlib getopt, the model of the getopts builtin the
bash scripts in bin/ use, and the checks getopt does NOT make are made after it:
each option may be given at most once, its value may not be empty or begin with
"-", and this script takes no positional arguments at all. A stray positional
exits 20, an unknown option 10, an option missing its value 11, and a malformed
value 22.

EVERYTHING this script prints goes to STDERR -- the progress (including a
per-archive download percentage), the final summary, and the load command. Its
product is files on disk, not a data stream, so a normal run leaves stdout EMPTY.
The ONE exception is `-h`: the help text is what a caller passing -h explicitly
asked for, so it is printed on stdout and the run exits 0.

It is OPERATOR-RUN: no ingest and no test ever fetches. A successful run PRINTS
the bin/load-external-data command to paste next, built from the files it
actually wrote and the POSITION each artifact declares, so the two positionals
are never put the wrong way round.

The run REFUSES to write an artifact that resolved to a member with ZERO data
rows, REFUSES a member whose header does not carry that artifact's identity
columns, REFUSES when a data-row count collapsed against the count
PROVENANCE.json recorded, and REFUSES when the resolved member NAME is not the
one PROVENANCE.json recorded. Nothing is written until all of them pass, and the
write itself is all-or-nothing across both extracted members AND the manifest.
"""

import csv
import hashlib
import io
import json
import posixpath
import re
import shlex
import sys
import zipfile
from datetime import datetime, timezone
from pathlib import Path
from typing import NamedTuple

# The shared python half of bin/functions (bin/pyfunctions.py): the EXIT_* band,
# the log-* family, fatal, the download stack and the post-getopt guard suite
# every fetcher needs. Resolved against THIS script's own directory rather than
# the caller's cwd, because these scripts are run from anywhere.
# ...and no bytecode cache beside it: importing a module normally writes
# bin/__pycache__/, and bin/ is a reviewed directory of scripts, not a package
# build tree. The import cost is microseconds; an untracked directory appearing
# in the working tree every time an operator runs a fetcher is a real cost.
sys.dont_write_bytecode = True
sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent))
from pyfunctions import (  # noqa: E402  (import after the sys.path bootstrap above)
    EXIT_INVALID_ARG_VALUE,
    EXIT_OPTION_REQUIRES_VALUE,
    HTTP_ERROR_BODY_PREVIEW_BYTES,
    bracketed,
    decode as shared_decode,
    fatal as shared_fatal,
    format_bytes,
    http_get as shared_http_get,
    log_error,
    log_info,
    log_warning,
    parse_short_options,
    write_atomically,
)


# Every refusal this script makes names the script. The fetchers all run under
# one orchestrator (bin/fetch-external-data), so a bare "[FATAL] fixtures
# directory does not exist" costs the reader a lookup to find out WHICH fetcher
# said it. pyfunctions' own refusals already carry the name; this gives the
# script-local ones the same prefix through one wrapper rather than 40 edited
# f-strings.
def fatal(message, status=1):
    shared_fatal(f"{SCRIPT_NAME}: {message}", status=status)


REPO_ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent
DEFAULT_OUTDIR = REPO_ROOT / "db" / "seed" / "scorecard"

# The page that names the current release. It is the ONLY discovery surface:
# https://ed-public-download.scorecard.network/downloads/ is a 404, so there is
# no directory listing to read, and the site's _payload.json carries the
# glossary rather than the download hrefs. The HTML is the source.
DATA_PAGE_URL = "https://collegescorecard.ed.gov/data/"
# The recorded stand-in an offline (-F) run reads that page from, so the
# discovery below is exercised by the shell harness with no network at all.
DATA_PAGE_FIXTURE = "scorecard-data-page-fixture.html"

MANIFEST_NAME = "PROVENANCE.json"

# The manifest's `source` vocabulary, named once. It is a PERSISTED guard
# vocabulary -- compared by == wherever the replay refusal and the route check
# read it back -- so a typo in one of those comparisons would silently downgrade
# a committed record from "protected" to "replaceable". Plain constants rather
# than an enum: the values are written to and read from JSON, and nothing here
# needs more than the names.
ROUTE_NETWORK = "network"
ROUTE_FIXTURES = "fixtures"
# Not a value ever WRITTEN: what manifest_route calls a manifest that declares
# neither, which joins the protected side.
ROUTE_UNKNOWN = "unknown"

# The zip entries macOS' own archiver adds beside the real member. The
# institution zip carries __MACOSX/._Most-Recent-Cohorts-Institution.csv -- a
# 226-byte resource fork whose name ends .csv, so an extension-based resolution
# would happily "find" it. Dropped by prefix (the directory entry) and by
# basename (the AppleDouble file itself), because the archiver writes both.
MACOSX_DIR_PREFIX = "__MACOSX/"
APPLEDOUBLE_PREFIX = "._"


class Artifact(NamedTuple):
    """One published Scorecard artifact, by NAME rather than by tuple position.

    url_name, member_name and fixture_name are three look-alike strings --
    "Most-Recent-Cohorts-Institution" appears in all three, differing only by a
    suffix -- and a positional unpacking that swapped two of them would still
    run: it would download the right archive and look for the wrong member.
    bin/fetch-ipeds and bin/fetch-cds-seed already state this house rule.

    url_name is BOTH the manifest key and the artifact half of the discovered
    URL. It carries no release stamp, deliberately: the stamp belongs to the
    RELEASE (one for the pair), not to either file, and it is discovered rather
    than declared.

    ingest_position is which POSITIONAL of bin/load-external-data /
    bin/ingest-colleges the written file becomes -- 1 is <institution.csv>, 2 is
    <fields.csv>. It is a declaration, and it is serialised into the manifest for
    the same reason bin/fetch-ipeds serialises ingest_option: a later reader that
    wants to build the load command must not re-derive the order from a filename.

    identity_columns is the SMALL set of header names that say "this is the file
    we think it is", and every row carries at least one column the other row's
    file does not. It is not the ingest's required-column contract
    (REQUIRED_INSTITUTION_COLUMNS, asserted in Kotlin before any write) -- see
    the module docstring for why duplicating that list here would be a second
    copy that drifts."""

    url_name: str
    member_name: str
    fixture_name: str
    ingest_position: int
    identity_columns: tuple[str, ...]


# The two artifacts, in the order bin/load-external-data takes them. Both are
# extracted from the SMALL per-file zips rather than the 448 MB
# College_Scorecard_Raw_Data_*.zip, which is a strict superset holding
# byte-identical copies of exactly these two members: 40 MB against 448 MB for
# the same bytes. (That archive also unpacks to a top directory stamped with a
# DIFFERENT date from its URL -- 06102026 vs 06032026 -- so a future variant that
# reached for it must resolve its top directory by pattern, never by
# reconstructing it from the URL stamp.)
#
# member_name is the name the extracted member is WRITTEN under, and it is the
# publisher's own name, so the load command printed at the end of a run is
# copy-pasteable. The fixture_name is the recorded stand-in an offline (-F) run
# accepts under that exact name, beside the stamped archive's own name.
ARTIFACTS = (
    Artifact(
        "Most-Recent-Cohorts-Institution",
        "Most-Recent-Cohorts-Institution.csv",
        "scorecard-institution-zip-fixture.zip",
        1,
        # PREDDEG is institution-ONLY. UNITID, INSTNM and CONTROL are all carried
        # by the published field-of-study header too (they are 3 of the 5 columns
        # the two files share), so a set built from those three alone ACCEPTS a
        # field-of-study member in this slot -- precisely the swap this guard
        # exists to refuse. The other direction already discriminated, because
        # CIPCODE and CREDLEV are absent from the institution header.
        ("UNITID", "INSTNM", "CONTROL", "PREDDEG"),
    ),
    Artifact(
        "Most-Recent-Cohorts-Field-of-Study",
        "Most-Recent-Cohorts-Field-of-Study.csv",
        "scorecard-field-of-study-zip-fixture.zip",
        2,
        ("UNITID", "CIPCODE", "CREDLEV"),
    ),
)

# The href shape the page serves, and the ONE place a download URL comes from.
# The URL that is fetched is the MATCHED TEXT, never a string assembled here:
# the unstamped institution download is three years stale and still answers 200,
# so a script that could build a URL could build that one.
#
# The artifact alternation is built FROM ARTIFACTS, so the name a match yields IS
# the key the rest of this script looks an artifact up by: the published name is
# spelled once, in the table above, and no site re-assembles it from a regex
# fragment plus a literal prefix. The two capture groups are that url_name and
# the eight-digit release stamp (MMDDYYYY as the publisher writes it).
#
# The href ENDS at the ".zip". Without the boundary the match is a PREFIX of a
# longer token -- "..._06102026.zip?ver=2" would yield "..._06102026.zip" -- and
# that is a URL this script ASSEMBLED by truncation, not one the page served. A
# query string, a signature or a ".zip.html" landing page is a publisher change
# for a human to read, exactly like a renamed file, so it matches nothing and is
# refused by require_one_download_each.
#
# The host is ed-public-download.scorecard.network. It MOVED: the
# ed-public-download.app.cloud.gov older prose in this tree names now 404s on
# everything including /, so an href pointing there is not a download this
# script silently accepts -- it simply does not match.
RELEASE_HREF_PATTERN = re.compile(
    r"https://ed-public-download\.scorecard\.network/downloads/"
    r"(" + "|".join(re.escape(artifact.url_name) for artifact in ARTIFACTS) + r")"
    r"_(\d{8})\.zip"
    r"(?=[\"'\s<])"
)

# The network bounds, the error-body preview bound and the whole download stack
# -- format_bytes, the progress reporter, the chunked read and the retrying
# http_get -- come from bin/pyfunctions.py, shared with the sibling fetchers.
# Nothing in them is Scorecard-specific, and a private copy here was a copy that
# could drift from the one every other fetcher downloads with.

# A re-fetched artifact carrying fewer than this fraction of the data rows the
# manifest recorded is treated as a failed extraction rather than a real release
# change: the Scorecard loses institutions in dozens, never in thousands.
SEED_SHRINK_FLOOR = 0.5


def http_get(url, label):
    """The shared retrying download (bin/pyfunctions.py), reporting through this
    script's own name so a refusal under bin/fetch-external-data still says
    which fetcher died."""
    return shared_http_get(url, label, SCRIPT_NAME)


def decode(body, filename):
    """The shared guarded decode (bin/pyfunctions.py), named for this script.

    utf-8-sig strips a BOM where there is one, so none survives into the UNITID
    header name the loaders match on, and a payload that is neither UTF-8 nor
    cp1252 is a refusal rather than a traceback out of the recovery path."""
    return shared_decode(body, filename, SCRIPT_NAME)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Release discovery
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


def read_data_page(fixtures):
    """(page text, origin): the /data/ page downloaded, or read from the
    recorded fixture when this is an offline (-F) replay, named by what was
    actually read.

    The origin is RETURNED rather than re-derived by the caller: it is the label
    every discovery refusal below carries, and a second branch deciding it could
    name a page this never opened.

    The page is read on BOTH routes rather than skipped offline, because the
    discovery below is this script's most publisher-dependent code and a replay
    that jumped straight to the recorded zips would exercise none of it.

    This SELECTS a route; the two readers below perform it, and the decode is
    stated once rather than per branch."""
    body, origin = read_page_fixture(fixtures) if fixtures is not None else download_data_page()
    return decode_page(body, origin), origin


def read_page_fixture(fixtures):
    """(page bytes, origin) from the recorded page, matched by its EXACT
    declared name, like every other fixture here."""
    path = fixtures / DATA_PAGE_FIXTURE
    log_info(f"Reading the release page fixture [{path}]")
    try:
        return path.read_bytes(), str(path)
    except FileNotFoundError as error:
        # The caught error is CARRIED, as read_manifest and read_member already
        # do here: ENOENT on a dangling symlink is reported by the OS about the
        # link's TARGET, and "no fixture in this directory" is a claim about the
        # directory that `ls` would then contradict.
        fatal(
            f"no release page fixture [{DATA_PAGE_FIXTURE}] in [{fixtures}] "
            f"([{type(error).__name__}] [{error}]); an offline replay discovers the release "
            f"from the recorded page exactly as a network run discovers it from "
            f"[{DATA_PAGE_URL}]"
        )


def download_data_page():
    """(page bytes, origin) from the live page, the ONLY discovery surface:
    /downloads/ is a 404, so there is no listing to read."""
    log_info(f"Discovering the current release from [{DATA_PAGE_URL}]")
    return http_get(DATA_PAGE_URL, "data page"), DATA_PAGE_URL


def decode_page(body, origin):
    """The page as text, REPORTING the bytes that were not text.

    errors="replace" is right here -- this is HTML being scanned for a URL
    shape, not data being ingested, so one undecodable byte in a page footer
    must not end a fetch, and what is MATCHED is still an exact ASCII pattern.
    But a body that is mostly replacement characters is a gzipped, cp1252 or
    captive-portal response rather than the release page, and the zero-match
    refusal below would otherwise blame the publisher for renaming a file."""
    page = body.decode("utf-8", errors="replace")
    replaced = page.count("\ufffd")
    if replaced:
        log_warning(
            f"[{origin}] carries [{replaced}] bytes that are not UTF-8, replaced for "
            f"matching; a body that is not text at all is an error page or a compressed "
            f"response, not the release page"
        )
    return page


class Download(NamedTuple):
    """One matched href: the URL the page served and the release stamp inside
    it.

    A NamedTuple because `url` and `stamp` are both strings read off the same
    match, and a positional unpacking that swapped them would still run -- it
    would compare URLs for release equality and try to download a stamp."""

    url: str
    stamp: str


def find_release_hrefs(page):
    """{url_name -> [Download]}: every stamped download the page serves, grouped
    by artifact.

    Reads the page and judges nothing. The two refusals below are what decide
    whether what it read is a release.

    The key is the CAPTURED url_name, not a name rebuilt from a prefix and a
    fragment: the alternation is built from ARTIFACTS, so what a match yields is
    already the key every artifact is looked up by."""
    downloads = {}
    for match in RELEASE_HREF_PATTERN.finditer(page):
        downloads.setdefault(match.group(1), []).append(Download(match.group(0), match.group(2)))
    return downloads


def page_preview(page):
    """The head of a page, on ONE line.

    Whitespace is collapsed because this goes into a [FATAL] message: the
    channel bin/scripts-tests and every operator grep on is line-oriented, and
    an HTML body pasted in raw would break one refusal across twenty lines.

    Bounded by HTTP_ERROR_BODY_PREVIEW_BYTES even though this slices CHARACTERS,
    not bytes: the number is a readability bound on one log line, not a memory
    bound, and one preview length for every body this script quotes is worth
    more than an exact unit."""
    return " ".join(page[:HTTP_ERROR_BODY_PREVIEW_BYTES].split())


def require_one_download_each(downloads, origin, page):
    """Refuses a page that offers no download for an artifact, or several.

    Zero matches is a publisher change -- a renamed file, a moved host, a page
    that stopped server-rendering its links -- and picking a fallback would mean
    fetching something nobody chose; several means which of them is current is a
    reading of the page this script does not make.

    BOTH artifacts are judged before anything is refused, the way
    require_data_rows, require_identity_columns and require_stable_baseline all
    do: a moved host renames both hrefs, and reporting only the first sends the
    operator round the publisher-change loop twice.

    The refusal quotes what the page DID offer and how long it was, because
    those are the facts that separate "one artifact renamed beside a matched
    sibling" from "this is not the /data/ page at all" -- an error page, a
    captive portal, a gzipped body or an empty one served with HTTP 200. On the
    network route the page is not retained, so it is quoted here or it is
    lost."""
    complaints = []
    for artifact in ARTIFACTS:
        matches = downloads.get(artifact.url_name, [])
        if not matches:
            offered = bracketed(downloads) if downloads else "no stamped download at all"
            complaints.append(
                f"[{origin}] names no download for [{artifact.url_name}] "
                f"(pattern [{RELEASE_HREF_PATTERN.pattern}]); the page read was "
                f"[{len(page)}] characters, it names {offered}, and it begins "
                f"[{page_preview(page)}]"
            )
        elif len(matches) > 1:
            complaints.append(
                f"[{origin}] names [{len(matches)}] downloads for [{artifact.url_name}] "
                f"{bracketed(download.url for download in matches)}, expected exactly one; "
                f"which of them is the current release is a reading of the page this script "
                f"does not make"
            )
    if complaints:
        fatal(
            f"{'; also '.join(complaints)}. The publisher renamed a file, moved the host or "
            f"stopped rendering the links, and which URL to fetch instead is a review, never "
            f"a guess. The unversioned URL is not a fallback: it still answers 200 with a "
            f"release from 2024"
        )


def require_one_release(downloads, origin):
    """The ONE release stamp both artifacts are offered from.

    Reached only AFTER require_one_download_each: that refusal is what makes
    downloads[artifact.url_name][0] safe here, since it has already established
    that every artifact has exactly one match. Called out of that order this
    raises KeyError instead of refusing.

    bin/ingest-colleges cross-reads the two files by UNITID, so a pair taken
    from two releases loads without error and is wrong. The stamp is never
    parsed as a date: it is an opaque identity compared for equality, and
    reading it as a date would invite a fetcher that reasons about which release
    is newer."""
    stamps = {artifact.url_name: downloads[artifact.url_name][0].stamp for artifact in ARTIFACTS}
    if len(set(stamps.values())) > 1:
        fatal(
            "the release stamps disagree ("
            + ", ".join(f"[{name}] is [{stamp}]" for name, stamp in stamps.items())
            + f"): [{origin}] is offering the two files from different releases, and "
            f"bin/ingest-colleges cross-reads them by UNITID, so a mismatched pair loads "
            f"without error and is wrong. Both halves come from ONE release or neither does"
        )
    return next(iter(stamps.values()))


def discover_release(page, origin):
    """(release, {url_name: url}) read off the /data/ page: matched, refused if
    the page does not offer exactly one download per artifact from one release,
    then reported.

    Both halves come from the SAME regex match: the URL that is downloaded is
    the href the publisher served, and the release stamp is the group inside it.
    Nothing here assembles a URL, which is the point -- the unstamped
    institution download answers 200 with the August 2024 release, so the
    ability to build a URL is the ability to build that one."""
    downloads = find_release_hrefs(page)
    require_one_download_each(downloads, origin, page)
    release = require_one_release(downloads, origin)
    # Indexed, not .get(): require_one_download_each above has already refused a
    # page offering zero or several downloads for an artifact, so exactly one
    # match per url_name exists by this line.
    urls = {artifact.url_name: downloads[artifact.url_name][0].url for artifact in ARTIFACTS}
    log_info(f"[{origin}] publishes release [{release}]")
    return release, urls


def report_release_change(outdir, previous, release, offline):
    """Says so when the discovered release is not the one PROVENANCE.json
    records -- and PROCEEDS.

    A new release is the whole POINT of this fetcher, so it is reported, never
    refused. That is the opposite stance from the member-drift guard below, and
    deliberately: a changed member NAME means the extraction may have taken the
    wrong file, while a changed release stamp means the publisher published, and
    an operator running the fetcher asked for exactly that.

    It is still worth a line. The row counts about to be measured against the
    recorded ones come from a DIFFERENT release, so this is the sentence that
    explains a legitimate shift in them.

    An offline (-F) replay reports nothing: the stamp it discovered came from
    the RECORDED page, so measuring it against a manifest would compare a
    fixture's release with a published one. The route is taken as an argument --
    the way require_offline_outdir, require_matching_route, build_manifest and
    print_summary all take it -- rather than read out of a nulled `release`,
    because `release` is exactly as real on a replay as on a fetch."""
    if offline or previous is None:
        return
    recorded = previous.get("release")
    if recorded == release:
        log_info(f"The release [{release}] is the one [{outdir / MANIFEST_NAME}] records")
        return
    log_info(
        f"The release moved: [{release}] now, [{recorded}] recorded in "
        f"[{outdir / MANIFEST_NAME}]. That is what this fetcher is for, so it proceeds -- the "
        f"guards below still measure this release's members and row counts against the "
        f"recorded ones"
    )


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reading the archives
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


def find_fixture(fixtures, artifact, url):
    """The one file in `fixtures` that stands in for `artifact`: either the
    published archive under the name the discovered href carries, or the
    recorded zip/CSV named in ARTIFACTS.

    Both are matched by EXACT name. A normalised substring search would be
    friendlier, but the fixture directory (college/src/test/resources) is shared
    with every other test in the repo, and it already holds unrelated files
    whose names begin "scorecard-"; this script does not own it, so one new file named by
    that directory's own convention would make a substring search ambiguous and
    this fetcher fatal, from a change with nothing to do with the Scorecard.
    Ambiguity is still fatal -- picking the first of two candidates would make
    the harness depend on readdir order -- but it now takes two files this table
    actually names.

    The stamped name is offered as a candidate so an operator can replay a REAL
    downloaded archive, whose name carries the release the recorded page
    declares, without renaming it. It is the BASENAME of the href the page
    served, not a name assembled here from the artifact and the stamp: the
    publisher's "<url_name>_<stamp>.zip" rule is declared once, by
    RELEASE_HREF_PATTERN, and re-typing it in a function body is how a rename
    splits the two."""
    published = posixpath.basename(url)
    candidates = [
        path
        for path in (fixtures / published, fixtures / artifact.fixture_name)
        if path.is_file()
    ]
    if not candidates:
        fatal(
            f"no fixture for [{artifact.url_name}] in [{fixtures}]: expected [{published}] "
            f"or [{artifact.fixture_name}]"
        )
    if len(candidates) > 1:
        fatal(
            f"[{len(candidates)}] fixtures stand in for [{artifact.url_name}] in "
            f"[{fixtures}]: {bracketed(path.name for path in candidates)}"
        )
    return candidates[0]


def is_macos_junk(member):
    """Whether a zip member is macOS archiver junk rather than published data.

    Most-Recent-Cohorts-Institution_*.zip ships
    __MACOSX/._Most-Recent-Cohorts-Institution.csv beside the real member: a
    226-byte AppleDouble resource fork whose name ends ".csv" and whose basename
    is the real member's basename with a "._" in front. It is filtered on BOTH
    facts the archiver writes, because either alone is a guess about the other's
    stability -- and what is being protected against is precisely a resolution
    that "finds" the resource fork and writes 226 bytes of binary as the
    institution file."""
    return member.startswith(MACOSX_DIR_PREFIX) or posixpath.basename(member).startswith(
        APPLEDOUBLE_PREFIX
    )


def resolve_member(archive, origin, expected_member):
    """The name of the one DATA member of an opened ARCHIVE, matched by EXACT
    name. (A raw payload has no member to resolve -- the body IS the member --
    so this is reached only for a zip.)

    The macOS junk is dropped FIRST, so it can neither be matched nor counted as
    "the only other member". Then the published name must be present exactly
    once: no member under it is as fatal as two of them, and the lone surviving
    member under an UNPUBLISHED name is never substituted in. That is the same
    rule bin/fetch-ipeds states, and here it has a live case rather than a
    hypothetical one -- the AppleDouble entry IS the wrong member sitting in the
    archive, so a "take whatever else is there" fallback would have a candidate
    every single run.

    The match is case-SENSITIVE, unlike IPEDS'. IPEDS ships its ARTIFACTS
    members under mixed casing and the casing is nobody's published rule; the Scorecard
    publishes two members whose names are stable and are the names this script
    writes, so a member differing only in case is a publisher change to look at,
    not a spelling to absorb."""
    members = [name for name in archive.namelist() if not is_macos_junk(name)]
    if not members:
        fatal(
            f"[{origin}] holds no member that is not macOS archiver junk "
            f"(members: {bracketed(archive.namelist())}); nothing in it is the published data"
        )
    named = [name for name in members if posixpath.basename(name) == expected_member]
    if len(named) == 1:
        return named[0]
    if len(named) > 1:
        fatal(
            f"[{origin}] holds [{len(named)}] members named [{expected_member}] "
            f"{bracketed(named)}, expected exactly one (all members: {bracketed(members)})"
        )
    fatal(
        f"[{origin}] holds no member named [{expected_member}]; its [{len(members)}] "
        f"non-junk members are {bracketed(members)}, and which of them is the release -- or "
        f"whether the publisher renamed it -- is a review, never a guess "
        f"(all members: {bracketed(archive.namelist())})"
    )


def read_member(body, origin, expected_member, url):
    """(member_body, member) for a payload: the one resolved member of a zip, or
    -- on the FIXTURE route only -- the body itself when the payload is already
    a plain CSV. A raw payload has no member to resolve, so member-name matching
    applies to zip members only, and the member recorded is the file that was
    read.

    member_body is BYTES, decoded and re-encoded UTF-8 first, so the digest
    describes the file the ingest reads and no BOM survives into the UNITID
    header name the loaders match on.

    Zip-ness is decided by reading the archive's structure (zipfile.is_zipfile),
    never by the extension -- which is what makes a fixture directory able to
    hold either form under the same name.

    On the NETWORK route a non-zip body is FATAL. Both published Scorecard
    artifacts are zips, so a body that is not one is an error page, a redirect
    notice or a captive-portal splash served with HTTP 200; treating it as the
    release would write a CSV of HTML and record its digest as provenance.

    A zip that will not open is reported through this script's own channel too. A
    truncated download is THE flaky-network failure here, and it must not arrive
    as a stdlib traceback naming a /nix/store path.

    The ROUTE is carried by `url`: a network fetch always has one, and `url is
    None` is what an offline (-F) replay means. That is the single value the
    raw-CSV branch below is allowed to open on."""
    if not zipfile.is_zipfile(io.BytesIO(body)):
        # url is None only on the FIXTURE route, so this is where -F accepts a
        # recorded CSV; a network body that is not a zip can never reach it.
        if url is not None:
            preview = body[:HTTP_ERROR_BODY_PREVIEW_BYTES].decode("utf-8", errors="replace")
            fatal(
                f"GET [{url}] returned [{len(body)}] bytes that are not a zip archive; both "
                f"published Scorecard artifacts are zips, so this is an error page or a "
                f"redirect served as a success, not the release. Body begins [{preview}]"
            )
        return decode(body, origin).encode("utf-8"), origin
    try:
        with zipfile.ZipFile(io.BytesIO(body)) as archive:
            member = resolve_member(archive, origin, expected_member)
            member_body = archive.read(member)
    except (zipfile.BadZipFile, EOFError, RuntimeError) as error:
        fatal(
            f"[{origin}] (from [{url or origin}], [{len(body)}] bytes) could not be read as a "
            f"zip archive: [{type(error).__name__}] [{error}]. A truncated or partial "
            f"download is the usual cause -- re-run the fetch; an encrypted or otherwise "
            f"unreadable archive is a publisher change and needs a look."
        )
    return decode(member_body, member).encode("utf-8"), member


def read_header_and_rows(member_body, origin, member):
    """(header, data rows) for an extracted member, from ONE parse.

    One walk, because the member is up to 153 MB and both facts come off the
    same reader: the header the identity guard checks, and the data-row count --
    header EXCLUDED -- the shrink guard reads. A line count would miscount both,
    since institution names carry embedded newlines inside quoted fields.

    A member with no header line AT ALL reports ([], 0), exactly as a
    header-only member reports (header, 0). Both are "the extraction produced no
    data", and there is one guard that refuses it (require_data_rows) rather
    than an exit from inside a constructor for one shape and a guard for the
    other.

    csv.Error is reported through this script's channel: this count is what the
    whole shrink guard rests on, so a member the reader cannot walk (a NUL byte,
    a field past the reader's limit) is a named refusal naming the archive, the
    member and the LINE the reader stopped on, not a traceback. The line number
    is free (the reader carries it) and it is the only locator anything will
    have: the member is held in memory and this run refuses to write it."""
    # newline="" is load-bearing: without it StringIO translates the newlines
    # inside quoted institution names and csv.reader sees extra rows. An
    # INFLATED count is the dangerous direction -- it can never trip the shrink
    # floor, so a miscount would pass every guard here.
    reader = csv.reader(io.StringIO(member_body.decode("utf-8"), newline=""))
    try:
        header = next(reader, None)
        if header is None:
            return [], 0
        return header, sum(1 for _ in reader)
    except csv.Error as error:
        fatal(
            f"[{origin}] -> [{member}] could not be read as CSV at line "
            f"[{reader.line_num}]: [{type(error).__name__}] [{error}]; the data-row count is "
            f"what the shrink guard compares against, so an unreadable member is refused "
            f"rather than counted as zero. The member is not written, so this line number is "
            f"the only locator there will be for it"
        )


class Source:
    """One Scorecard artifact: the archive it came from, the member taken out of
    it, and the bytes about to be written. A value: everything it reports is
    passed in or computed from the bytes, and nothing here can exit."""

    def __init__(self, artifact, url, body, member, member_body, header, rows, fetched_at):
        self.artifact = artifact
        self.url_name = artifact.url_name
        self.output_name = artifact.member_name
        self.url = url
        self.bytes = len(body)
        self.sha256 = hashlib.sha256(body).hexdigest()
        self.member = member
        # The member exactly as it will be written: decoded, BOM stripped and
        # re-encoded UTF-8. The digest describes the file that lands, not the
        # member as it sat compressed in the archive. The decoded TEXT is
        # deliberately not retained -- the field-of-study member alone is ~153 MB
        # of it, and nothing reads it after the header and the row count.
        self.member_body = member_body
        self.member_bytes = len(member_body)
        self.member_sha256 = hashlib.sha256(member_body).hexdigest()
        self.header = header
        self.rows = rows
        self.fetched_at = fetched_at

    def as_json(self):
        """The recorded fact sheet for one artifact.

        `ingest_position` is this table's declaration of WHICH POSITIONAL the
        written member becomes -- 1 is <institution.csv>, 2 is <fields.csv> --
        and it is serialised for the reason bin/fetch-ipeds serialises
        ingest_option: a later reader that builds the load command must read the
        order the fetcher declared, not re-derive it from a filename. The two
        positionals are interchangeable to the shell and are not interchangeable
        to bin/ingest-colleges, and nothing downstream would report a swap.

        The RELEASE is not repeated here. It belongs to the pair, is checked to
        be one value for both artifacts before anything is downloaded, and is
        recorded once at the top level; a per-artifact copy could only ever
        disagree with it."""
        return {
            "url": self.url,
            "fetched_at": self.fetched_at,
            "bytes": self.bytes,
            "sha256": self.sha256,
            "member": self.member,
            "member_bytes": self.member_bytes,
            "member_sha256": self.member_sha256,
            "rows": self.rows,
            "ingest_position": self.artifact.ingest_position,
        }


def load_sources(fixtures, urls, fetched_at):
    """{url_name -> Source} for both artifacts, downloaded from the DISCOVERED
    urls, or read from `fixtures` with no network at all. Nothing is written
    here: the CSVs land only once the header and the counts they carry have been
    believed.

    The header and the row count are taken HERE, beside the decode that produced
    the text, so that a member which yields neither is an ordinary Source
    carrying rows=0 and is refused by a guard like any other, rather than by an
    exit from inside a constructor."""
    sources = {}
    for index, artifact in enumerate(ARTIFACTS, start=1):
        # An [index/total] prefix on every line of one artifact's report, so a
        # run that is being watched says how far through ARTIFACTS it is, and a
        # captured log can be read back per artifact.
        step = f"[{index}/{len(ARTIFACTS)}]"
        if fixtures is not None:
            path = find_fixture(fixtures, artifact, urls[artifact.url_name])
            log_info(f"{step} [{artifact.url_name}] reading fixture [{path}]")
            body = path.read_bytes()
            # None IS the fixture route: read_member and the manifest both read
            # the absence of a url as "this was not downloaded".
            url = None
            # The fixture's own name, so PROVENANCE names the file actually read
            # rather than the artifact it stands in for.
            origin = path.name
        else:
            url = urls[artifact.url_name]
            log_info(f"{step} [{artifact.url_name}] downloading [{url}]")
            body = http_get(url, artifact.url_name)
            origin = posixpath.basename(url)
        member_body, member = read_member(body, origin, artifact.member_name, url)
        header, rows = read_header_and_rows(member_body, origin, member)
        # The facts the guards below are about to decide on -- the member that
        # was resolved, how big it is, how many columns it declares and how many
        # data rows it holds -- reported as they are learned rather than only
        # when one of them fails.
        log_info(
            f"{step} [{artifact.url_name}] -> member [{member}] "
            f"[{format_bytes(len(member_body))}] [{len(header)}] columns [{rows}] data rows"
        )
        sources[artifact.url_name] = Source(
            artifact,
            url,
            body,
            member,
            member_body,
            header,
            rows,
            fetched_at,
        )
    return sources


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The manifest this run would overwrite
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


def read_manifest(outdir):
    """The parsed manifest OBJECT in `outdir`, or None where there is no file.

    ONE loader with ONE corruption policy, because both readers of this file --
    the offline-replay refusal and the shrink guard -- are protecting the same
    thing. A manifest that cannot be read is not an absent manifest: it is the
    baseline and the provenance record, damaged. Neither caller may quietly
    proceed past it, which is why this fatals rather than returning None.

    "Absent" is decided by the READ failing with FileNotFoundError, not by a
    prior exists() probe: a path whose parent is a file (ENOTDIR), a symlink loop
    (ELOOP) or an unreadable directory all make exists() answer False, and every
    one of those would disarm the replay refusal on a directory that does hold a
    manifest.

    A parsed value that is not an OBJECT is corruption, not absence. A file
    holding the literal `null` parses fine and is falsy, and treating it as "no
    manifest" would step straight past the refusal and overwrite the reviewed
    record; a list or a scalar would instead raise AttributeError from whichever
    reader touched it first. Both are the same fault and get the same refusal.

    A file that is not UTF-8 TEXT is the same damage one level lower.
    read_text() decodes, and UnicodeDecodeError is a ValueError, so it is caught
    by neither the OSError clause here nor main's OSError boundary: one stray
    byte would leave this script as a stdlib traceback naming a /nix/store path,
    which is the outcome this whole channel exists to prevent.

    The exception TYPE is reported beside its message throughout: "cannot open",
    "is not UTF-8" and "is not JSON" need different repairs."""
    path = outdir / MANIFEST_NAME
    try:
        raw = path.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return None
    except OSError as error:
        fatal(f"[{path}] is unreadable: [{type(error).__name__}] [{error}]. {_MANIFEST_REPAIR}")
    except UnicodeDecodeError as error:
        fatal(
            f"[{path}] is unreadable, it is not UTF-8 text ([{error.reason}] at byte "
            f"[{error.start}]); every run that writes this file writes UTF-8, so a byte that "
            f"is not is damage. {_MANIFEST_REPAIR}"
        )
    try:
        manifest = json.loads(raw)
    except ValueError as error:
        fatal(
            f"[{path}] is unreadable, it is not JSON: [{type(error).__name__}] [{error}]. "
            f"{_MANIFEST_REPAIR}"
        )
    if not isinstance(manifest, dict):
        fatal(
            f"[{path}] is unreadable: it parses as [{type(manifest).__name__}], not a JSON "
            f"object. An empty or `null` manifest is DAMAGE, never an absent one -- reading "
            f"it as absent is exactly how a reviewed record gets overwritten. "
            f"{_MANIFEST_REPAIR}"
        )
    require_agreeing_route(path, manifest)
    return manifest


def require_agreeing_route(path, manifest):
    """Refuses a manifest whose two statements of its own route disagree.

    build_manifest writes `source` and `fixture_derived` from the ONE `offline`
    flag, so exactly two combinations are ever written. Which route made this
    record is what decides whether it may be overwritten, and reading that off a
    file that says both would rest the protection of a committed record on one
    field of two agreeing unchecked -- so `fixture_derived` is READ BACK here
    rather than only written.

    A record declaring the replay route must declare it with BOTH fields: that
    is the reading which leaves a committed record replaceable, and a hand edit
    (this file invites one) that sets one field and not the other must not reach
    it. Both values are named, because which of the two was edited is the
    repair."""
    declared = manifest.get("source")
    fixture_derived = manifest.get("fixture_derived")
    if (declared == ROUTE_FIXTURES) != (fixture_derived is True):
        fatal(
            f"[{path}] declares [source={declared}] beside "
            f"[fixture_derived={fixture_derived}], and those two contradict each other: one "
            f"manifest states its route twice, and every run writes them together. Which "
            f"route made this record decides whether it may be overwritten, and it is not "
            f"read off a file that says both. {_MANIFEST_REPAIR}"
        )


_MANIFEST_REPAIR = (
    "It is both the record of the fetch that produced this directory and the baseline "
    "this run would be measured against, so nothing here can proceed past it: repair it, "
    "or point -o at a different directory."
)


def manifest_route(manifest):
    """(route, declared) for a parsed manifest: one of ROUTE_NETWORK,
    ROUTE_FIXTURES or ROUTE_UNKNOWN, and the value the manifest actually
    declares.

    EXHAUSTIVE on purpose. Reading `source == "fixtures"` and treating everything
    else as a fetch would be equally safe, but reading `source == "network"` and
    treating everything else as replaceable is not: a manifest carrying real
    fetched_at/bytes/sha256 and simply missing the optional `source` key would be
    read as a replay and overwritten by one. Safety must not depend on a key
    being present, so the third case is named and joins the protected side (see
    require_offline_outdir).

    The DECLARED value comes back with the classification because it is the
    diagnosis. A manifest declaring "netwrok" is not a manifest that declares
    nothing, and a refusal saying so sends the operator looking for a missing
    key instead of at the typo in front of them.

    `source` alone is enough HERE only because read_manifest has already refused
    a manifest whose `source` and `fixture_derived` disagree
    (require_agreeing_route): the replay route is declared by both fields or by
    neither, so no record classified below states its route twice."""
    declared = manifest.get("source")
    if declared in (ROUTE_NETWORK, ROUTE_FIXTURES):
        return declared, declared
    return ROUTE_UNKNOWN, declared


def get_manifest_artifacts(manifest):
    """The manifest's `artifacts` object, validated.

    Both baseline readers walk this, so its SHAPE is checked once here rather
    than assumed twice: a list-valued `artifacts`, or an entry that is not an
    object, would otherwise crash whichever reader reached it first with an
    AttributeError instead of this script's refusal.

    ABSENCE is damage too, not an empty baseline. build_manifest always writes
    the key, so a manifest without it is a record that lost part of itself, and
    defaulting it to {} would leave the run measuring against nothing, both
    guards reporting NOT armed, and the reviewed record overwritten with exit 0 --
    the same fault read_manifest refuses one level up. No default: None is not a
    dict, so an absent key falls into the refusal already written below."""
    artifacts = manifest.get("artifacts")
    if not isinstance(artifacts, dict):
        fatal(
            f"the manifest's [artifacts] is a [{type(artifacts).__name__}], not a JSON "
            f"object keyed by artifact name; the baseline cannot be read from it. "
            f"{_MANIFEST_REPAIR}"
        )
    wrong = sorted(
        f"[{name}] is a [{type(entry).__name__}]"
        for name, entry in artifacts.items()
        if not isinstance(entry, dict)
    )
    if wrong:
        fatal(
            f"the manifest records {', '.join(wrong)} under [artifacts], not a JSON object; "
            f"the baseline cannot be read from it. {_MANIFEST_REPAIR}"
        )
    return artifacts


def get_baseline(manifest, field):
    """{url_name -> the recorded `field`} -- only for artifacts that RECORD one.

    ONE accessor parameterised by the field, because the field name is the only
    thing that varies between the two baselines this script keeps (`rows` and
    `member`) and a third recorded field would otherwise be a third copy of the
    same comprehension.

    An absent key is an absent entry. Defaulting it to None here and then asking
    isinstance at every call site puts the same policy in three places, and a
    site that forgot it would compare against None."""
    return {
        name: entry[field]
        for name, entry in get_manifest_artifacts(manifest).items()
        if field in entry
    }


def is_usable_row_count(rows):
    """Whether a recorded row count can be a baseline.

    bool is rejected explicitly: isinstance(True, int) is True in Python, so a
    manifest recording `"rows": true` would otherwise arm the shrink guard
    against a baseline of 1. A count of zero or less is not a release either, and
    a floor computed from it can never fire."""
    return isinstance(rows, int) and not isinstance(rows, bool) and rows > 0


def is_usable_member_name(member):
    """Whether a recorded member name can be a baseline.

    An empty string is refused rather than compared: it is a name no archive
    publishes, so a manifest carrying one would arm the member-drift guard
    against a value every real member differs from, and every run afterwards
    would refuse a correct extraction. A non-string is damage the guard has
    nothing to compare against at all."""
    return isinstance(member, str) and member != ""


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The plausibility guards, in the order main runs them
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


def require_data_rows(outdir, sources):
    """Refuses an extraction that produced no data.

    Fatal on its own, with or without a baseline: an empty CSV is never a real
    Scorecard release, and a first run has no committed counts to be caught by.

    A zero-byte member and a header-only member both count zero rows, but they
    are NOT the same operator action -- one is a missing or truncated file to
    re-fetch, the other is a real file whose data rows are gone -- so each is
    named for what it is."""
    log_info(f"Checking both artifacts produced data rows ([{len(sources)}] artifacts)")
    empty = sorted(
        f"[{source.url_name}] -> [{source.member}] "
        + (
            "is zero bytes (nothing was extracted)"
            if source.member_bytes == 0
            else f"holds a header and no data rows ([{source.member_bytes}] bytes)"
        )
        for source in sources.values()
        if source.rows == 0
    )
    if empty:
        fatal(
            f"{', '.join(empty)}; produced no data rows, so refusing to write the "
            f"extracted CSVs in [{outdir}]"
        )


# How much of a refused header is quoted back. The institution file declares
# 3,308 columns, and the swap this guard exists to refuse is decided in the first
# handful -- the two published headers diverge at CIPCODE and CREDLEV. The member
# is never written (every guard runs before write_atomically), so what is quoted
# is the only surviving copy of the value that failed.
IDENTITY_HEADER_PREVIEW_COLUMNS = 8


def header_preview(header):
    """The first column names of a header, in PUBLISHED order.

    Not bracketed(): that SORTS, and the order the publisher wrote the columns in
    is half of what identifies the file -- the institution header opens
    UNITID,OPEID,... and the field-of-study one does not."""
    shown = ", ".join(f"[{name}]" for name in header[:IDENTITY_HEADER_PREVIEW_COLUMNS])
    if len(header) <= IDENTITY_HEADER_PREVIEW_COLUMNS:
        return shown
    return f"{shown}, ... ([{len(header) - IDENTITY_HEADER_PREVIEW_COLUMNS}] more)"


def require_identity_columns(outdir, sources):
    """Refuses a member whose header does not carry that artifact's identity
    columns.

    This asks ONE question: is this the file we think it is? The institution
    file declares UNITID/INSTNM/CONTROL/PREDDEG and the field-of-study file
    declares UNITID/CIPCODE/CREDLEV, so the two are told apart by their headers
    and not by the name of the zip they arrived in. That is the fetch-time
    failure worth catching, because the pair is loaded as two POSITIONALS: a
    swap survives every check made of a filename.

    The subset test is one-directional, so each set must hold a column the OTHER
    file does not carry, or half the swap passes: the published field-of-study
    header carries UNITID, INSTNM and CONTROL as well, and PREDDEG is what it
    does not.

    It is deliberately NOT the ingest's required-column contract. The Kotlin
    loaders assert REQUIRED_INSTITUTION_COLUMNS and REQUIRED_FIELDS_COLUMNS
    before any write (RFC 139); re-typing those lists here would be a second
    copy that drifts silently the day a column is added to the first, and it
    would move the ingest's contract into a script the ingest does not run.

    No column COUNT is asserted either. 3,308 and 178 are today's numbers and
    the publisher ADDS columns; a count assertion would turn a routine release
    into a fetch failure, which is the opposite of what a guard is for.

    The refusal QUOTES the header it refused, bounded. That header is the value
    that failed and it is the one fact separating "the two positionals were
    swapped" from "the publisher renamed a column"; the member is never written,
    so a refusal that dropped it would destroy the evidence permanently."""
    log_info("Checking each member's header carries that artifact's identity columns")
    missing = sorted(
        f"[{source.url_name}] -> [{source.member}] has [{len(source.header)}] columns "
        f"beginning {header_preview(source.header)} and is missing "
        f"{bracketed(set(source.artifact.identity_columns) - set(source.header))}"
        for source in sources.values()
        if not set(source.artifact.identity_columns) <= set(source.header)
    )
    if missing:
        fatal(
            f"{', '.join(missing)}; a member without those columns is not the file this "
            f"artifact names -- the two positionals may have been swapped, or the publisher "
            f"renamed a column the loaders match on -- so refusing to write the extracted "
            f"CSVs in [{outdir}]"
        )


def armed_guards(outdir, previous, sources):
    """(rows_armed, member_armed): whether EACH baseline guard is armed for every
    artifact. It also REPORTS as it goes -- one log_error per guard that is not
    armed, naming the artifacts and the recorded value that disarmed it -- so a
    caller reads the verdict and the operator reads the reason.

    There are two independent guards and they must be reported independently. A
    manifest recording `rows` but no usable `member` disarms the MEMBER guard --
    the only check that can see a member swap, since the AppleDouble entry and a
    renamed member say nothing about a row count -- and a run that armed on
    `rows` alone would print a fully guarded success while that hole was open.

    Three ways to be unarmed, and the quiet ones are the worse. With NO manifest
    at all there is no baseline for anything, and a run with no protection must
    not print what a protected run prints. With a manifest whose artifact keys
    this run never sees the baseline is keyed by names that no longer exist. With
    a recorded value of the wrong TYPE (a row count as a string, a member as
    null) the key is there and the baseline still is not -- and the offending
    value is printed, because it is the diagnosis."""
    manifest_path = outdir / MANIFEST_NAME
    if previous is None:
        log_error(
            f"[{manifest_path}] does not exist, so NEITHER baseline guard is armed for any "
            f"artifact: this run's row counts and resolved members are believed as they "
            f"are. That is how a first generation is made -- but against a directory that "
            f"should already hold a manifest it means the baseline was lost."
        )
        return False, False
    return (
        _warn_unarmed(
            manifest_path,
            sources,
            get_baseline(previous, "rows"),
            is_usable_row_count,
            "row count",
            "the shrink guard is NOT armed",
        ),
        _warn_unarmed(
            manifest_path,
            sources,
            get_baseline(previous, "member"),
            is_usable_member_name,
            "member name",
            "the member-drift guard is NOT armed -- a renamed or substituted member carries "
            "its own row count, so nothing else can see it",
        ),
    )


def _warn_unarmed(manifest_path, sources, recorded, usable, baseline_label, consequence):
    """Whether one baseline covers every artifact, reporting per artifact it does
    not and naming the value that made it unusable.

    The value is printed beside its TYPE rather than as a repr: a row count
    recorded as the string "3" and one recorded as 3 must stay distinguishable,
    and so must a member recorded as null from one recorded as "None", but a
    repr wraps the value in the single quotes this project's messages never
    use."""
    unarmed = sorted(
        f"[{source.url_name}] (no {baseline_label} recorded)"
        if source.url_name not in recorded
        else f"[{source.url_name}] (records {baseline_label} [{recorded[source.url_name]}], "
        f"a [{type(recorded[source.url_name]).__name__}])"
        for source in sources.values()
        if not usable(recorded.get(source.url_name))
    )
    if not unarmed:
        return True
    log_error(f"[{manifest_path}]: {', '.join(unarmed)}; {consequence}.")
    return False


def list_member_drift(manifest_path, previous, sources):
    """The member-drift complaints, if any.

    Catches a wrong member resolution, which the row count CANNOT: a member
    resolved out of the same archive under a different name carries whatever
    rows it carries, and nothing about the count says which file was read. The
    manifest records `member`; this is what reads it back."""
    recorded = get_baseline(previous, "member")
    drifted = [
        f"[{source.url_name}]: [{source.member}] now, [{recorded[source.url_name]}] recorded"
        for source in sources.values()
        if is_usable_member_name(recorded.get(source.url_name))
        and recorded[source.url_name] != source.member
    ]
    if not drifted:
        return []
    return [
        f"the resolved member changed against [{manifest_path}] ({'; '.join(drifted)}); "
        f"a renamed or substituted member says nothing about a row count, so this is the "
        f"only check that sees it"
    ]


def list_row_shrink(manifest_path, previous, sources):
    """The shrink complaints, if any.

    Catches a truncated or collapsed read: an extraction that succeeded and
    produced a fraction of the release. It compares DATA-ROW COUNTS per
    artifact, not zip bytes and not CSV bytes -- counts are immune to encoding
    drift, where a BOM appearing or disappearing moves the byte count by three
    and the row count by zero. The three sibling fetchers compare counts (rows,
    codes) for the same reason."""
    recorded = get_baseline(previous, "rows")
    shrunk = [
        f"[{source.url_name}]: [{source.rows}] now, [{recorded[source.url_name]}] recorded"
        for source in sources.values()
        if is_usable_row_count(recorded.get(source.url_name))
        and source.rows < SEED_SHRINK_FLOOR * recorded[source.url_name]
    ]
    if not shrunk:
        return []
    return [f"row counts collapsed against [{manifest_path}] ({'; '.join(shrunk)})"]


def require_stable_baseline(outdir, previous, sources):
    """Refuses to overwrite a recorded extraction with an implausible one.

    Two independent comparisons -- the member NAME and the row COUNT -- because
    they catch different failures. Both are run and BOTH verdicts are collected
    into one refusal: an extraction that drifted its member and collapsed its
    counts has two things wrong with it, and reporting only the first sends the
    operator round the loop twice.

    A new RELEASE is not one of the failures. The release stamp changing is
    reported by report_release_change and proceeds; what these two compare is
    whether THIS release's extraction looks like a release at all."""
    if previous is None:
        return
    manifest_path = outdir / MANIFEST_NAME
    log_info(f"Checking the resolved member names against [{manifest_path}]")
    complaints = list_member_drift(manifest_path, previous, sources)
    log_info(f"Checking the row counts against [{manifest_path}]")
    complaints += list_row_shrink(manifest_path, previous, sources)
    if complaints:
        fatal(
            f"{'; also '.join(complaints)}. Refusing to overwrite the extracted CSVs in "
            f"[{outdir}]."
        )


def require_matching_route(outdir, previous, offline):
    """A real (network) fetch is refused against a directory whose manifest
    records a FIXTURE replay.

    Without this the run reaches the baseline guards and fails there, and the
    message it fails with is a lie: every real member name is compared against a
    fixture CSV's name, so the member-drift guard blames a renamed member, and
    the real row counts are compared against three. The fault is the ROUTE, and
    the route is knowable before a byte is fetched.

    (The mirror case -- an offline replay against a real record -- is
    require_offline_outdir, which refuses for a different reason: there it is the
    committed provenance that must not be overwritten.)"""
    if offline or previous is None:
        return
    route, declared = manifest_route(previous)
    if route != ROUTE_FIXTURES:
        return
    manifest_path = outdir / MANIFEST_NAME
    fatal(
        f"[{manifest_path}] declares [source={declared}] (a [{type(declared).__name__}]): it "
        f"is the record of an offline (-F) replay, not of a fetch. Its members are fixture "
        f"file names and its row counts are a handful of rows, so measuring a real download "
        f"against it would report a member drift and a row collapse that are neither -- the "
        f"ROUTE changed. Fetch into a directory whose manifest records a real fetch, or into "
        f"an empty one."
    )


def require_offline_outdir(outdir, previous, offline):
    """An offline (-F) replay is REFUSED against an output directory whose
    manifest is not itself a fixture replay, before anything at all is written.

    PROVENANCE.json is the only committed record of the real download and the
    shrink baseline every later fetch is measured against. Writing fixture CSVs
    beside it would leave a directory whose manifest describes the real release
    and whose files are a three-row replay -- silently inconsistent, and worse
    than either half alone. So the run stops here rather than writing some of
    it: a replay wants its own -o.

    A manifest that declares NOTHING is refused with the same force as one that
    declares a fetch (manifest_route): the protection may not depend on an
    optional key being present in a file this script did not necessarily write.
    The refusal NAMES what was declared -- "netwrok" is a typo the operator can
    fix, and a message saying "declares no source" would hide it."""
    if not offline or previous is None:
        return
    route, declared = manifest_route(previous)
    if route == ROUTE_FIXTURES:
        return
    manifest_path = outdir / MANIFEST_NAME
    claim = (
        f"declares [source={declared}] (a [{type(declared).__name__}]) and so records a "
        f"real fetch"
        if route == ROUTE_NETWORK
        else f"declares [source={declared}] (a [{type(declared).__name__}]), which is not "
        f"[{ROUTE_NETWORK}] or [{ROUTE_FIXTURES}], so it is read as one that records a real "
        f"fetch"
    )
    fatal(
        f"[{manifest_path}] {claim}; an offline (-F) replay refuses to write into that "
        f"directory at all -- its CSVs would not be the ones the manifest describes. Give "
        f"the replay its own output directory with -o."
    )


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Writing
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


def build_manifest(sources, offline, release, fetched_at):
    """The manifest: which release it is, when and from where each archive came,
    its size and digest, which member was taken out of it, that member's digest
    as written, the data-row count the shrink guard reads next time, and the
    positional the member is loaded as.

    This file is the ONLY committed record of the fetch -- the CSVs themselves
    are far too large to commit -- so an OFFLINE (-F) run is refused outright
    against a directory that holds one (require_offline_outdir, before anything
    is written).

    Where an offline run DOES write one -- a fresh output directory, which is
    what the shell harness uses -- it is marked unmistakably: fixture_derived
    true, source "fixtures", every fetched_at null, and release null. `offline`
    is the ONE input that decides source and fixture_derived, and `release` is
    the value main derived from that same flag ONCE and handed to every consumer
    that reports what this run got, so the manifest and the summary line cannot
    disagree about it. `fetched_at` is likewise the caller's single timestamp,
    stamped identically at both levels: asking the clock again here would make
    the top-level field later than every artifact by the whole download.

    The RELEASE is top level because it is a property of the PAIR: discovery has
    already refused two artifacts whose stamps disagree, so one recorded value
    cannot contradict the files beside it. It is the only version identity the
    publisher gives these files -- there is no survey year here -- and recording
    it is what lets the next run say the release moved instead of silently
    fetching a different one."""
    return {
        "fetched_at": fetched_at,
        "source": ROUTE_FIXTURES if offline else ROUTE_NETWORK,
        "fixture_derived": offline,
        "release": release,
        "artifacts": {source.url_name: source.as_json() for source in sources.values()},
    }


def print_summary(outdir, sources, offline, release, rows_armed, member_armed):
    """The one success line, reporting each guard as it actually stood.

    `release` is the release this run FETCHED -- null on an offline replay, the
    same value the manifest records -- not the stamp discovery read off the
    page.

    The guard state is stated PER GUARD. A single "guarded"/"not guarded" verdict
    over two independent checks either overstates a run whose member baseline was
    missing, or understates one whose only gap was a renamed artifact -- and this
    line is the whole record an operator has of which protection was in force."""
    counts = " ".join(f"[{source.output_name}]=[{source.rows}]" for source in sources.values())
    route = ROUTE_FIXTURES if offline else ROUTE_NETWORK
    guards = (
        f"shrink: {'armed' if rows_armed else 'NOT armed'}, "
        f"member: {'armed' if member_armed else 'NOT armed'}"
    )
    # `release` is the value that was FETCHED, derived once in main, so this line
    # and the manifest beside it cannot disagree: a replay read its stamp off a
    # RECORDED page, and naming it here would claim a published release this run
    # never fetched.
    #
    # Decided from the ROUTE, which is the fact being reported and is a parameter
    # of this function -- not from `release is None`, which would make a null
    # carry "this was a replay" and print "an offline replay" about a network
    # fetch whose stamp somehow went missing.
    named = "an offline replay (no release)" if offline else f"release [{release}]"
    log_info(
        f"College Scorecard {named} written to [{outdir}] from [{route}]; "
        f"guards [{guards}]: {counts}"
    )


def print_load_command(outdir, sources):
    """The bin/load-external-data command to paste next, built from the files
    this run actually wrote.

    The ORDER comes from each artifact's declared ingest_position, not from the
    order they happen to sit in ARTIFACTS and not from their names: the two
    paths are positionals, so swapping them produces a run that loads the
    field-of-study file as the institution file and fails nine phases in. Sorting
    on the declaration means the printed command is right because the table says
    so.

    Every path is shell-quoted (shlex.quote, as bin/remote does), because -o may
    legitimately name "$HOME/Google Drive/scorecard" and an unquoted path there
    prints a command bash splits into two arguments."""
    ordered = sorted(sources.values(), key=lambda source: source.artifact.ingest_position)
    paths = " ".join(shlex.quote(str(outdir / source.output_name)) for source in ordered)
    log_info("Feed them to the ingest with:")
    log_info(f"  nix develop -c bin/load-external-data {paths}")


# The name this script reports itself as; every refusal names it, because the
# fetchers all run under one wrapper and a message must say which refused.
SCRIPT_NAME = "fetch-scorecard"

# The option grammar, in getopt's own notation and declared ONCE: this is what
# pyfunctions.parse_short_options parses argv with, and what it derives the
# value-taking letters from. A second hand-written list of those letters is a
# declaration that can drift from the one getopt actually uses.
GETOPT_SPEC = "o:F:h"


def parse_args(argv):
    """(outdir, fixtures) from argv, fataling on a malformed option.

    The grammar, the -h answer and every value-shape guard getopt cannot make --
    a repeated option, an empty value, a value that is really the next option --
    live in bin/pyfunctions.py, shared with the sibling fetchers. What is
    decided HERE is what only this script knows:

    -F REQUIRES -o. Without it, an offline replay defaults to db/seed/scorecard
    -- the committed record -- and whether it is stopped then depends on what is
    inside a data file: a missing manifest, or one already marked `fixtures`, and
    the replay writes over the committed seed. require_offline_outdir stays as
    the second line of defence for an explicit -o, but the argument shape is
    decided by the arguments.

    And an -o naming something that exists and is not a directory is a usage
    error, judged as an ARGUMENT before anything reads a disk, rather than the
    traceback mkdir would raise."""
    given = parse_short_options(argv, GETOPT_SPEC, __doc__, SCRIPT_NAME)

    outdir = Path(given["-o"]) if "-o" in given else DEFAULT_OUTDIR
    fixtures = Path(given["-F"]) if "-F" in given else None
    if fixtures is not None and "-o" not in given:
        fatal(
            f"option [-F] requires an explicit [-o]: an offline replay must not be aimed "
            f"at the default output directory [{DEFAULT_OUTDIR}], which holds the "
            f"committed record of the real fetch",
            status=EXIT_OPTION_REQUIRES_VALUE,
        )
    if outdir.exists() and not outdir.is_dir():
        fatal(
            f"option [-o] names [{outdir}], which exists and is not a directory; the output "
            f"directory cannot be created there",
            status=EXIT_INVALID_ARG_VALUE,
        )
    return outdir, fixtures


def main(argv):
    outdir, fixtures = parse_args(argv)
    offline = fixtures is not None
    if offline and not fixtures.is_dir():
        # Reported by what is actually THERE. is_dir() is false for an existing
        # FILE as well as for nothing at all, and "does not exist" sends an
        # operator who pointed -F at a zip to check their spelling instead of at
        # the mistake they made. It is the same distinction parse_args already
        # draws for -o, three lines up.
        fatal(
            f"fixtures directory [{fixtures}] "
            + (
                "exists and is not a directory"
                if fixtures.exists()
                else "does not exist"
            )
        )

    # Read once, under one corruption policy, before anything is written --
    # including the directory itself.
    previous = read_manifest(outdir)
    require_offline_outdir(outdir, previous, offline)
    require_matching_route(outdir, previous, offline)
    outdir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)

    # The start line: the ROUTE (the discovery page, or the fixture directory a
    # replay reads) and the directory this will write. Both are decisions the
    # arguments just made, and both are what an operator checks before letting a
    # ~250 MB extraction run.
    route = f"fixtures [{fixtures}]" if offline else f"network [{DATA_PAGE_URL}]"
    log_info(f"Fetching the College Scorecard pair from {route} into [{outdir}]")

    # Discovery FIRST, and on both routes: it decides which URLs are fetched at
    # all, and a page that no longer names the pair must stop the run before a
    # 23 MB download rather than after it.
    page, page_origin = read_data_page(fixtures)
    release, urls = discover_release(page, page_origin)
    # The stamp DISCOVERED is not the release FETCHED. A replay discovers one --
    # the recorded page names it, and find_fixture matches a stamped archive by
    # it -- and publishes none, so everything that REPORTS what this run got is
    # handed the second value. Derived here, once: the same "None if offline"
    # written at each consumer is one a consumer can forget, and one of them did.
    fetched_release = None if offline else release
    report_release_change(outdir, previous, release, offline)

    fetched_at = None if offline else datetime.now(timezone.utc).isoformat(timespec="seconds")
    sources = load_sources(fixtures, urls, fetched_at)
    # Nothing is written until the extraction is believed: a fetch that succeeded
    # and a member resolution that went wrong must leave the recorded CSVs and
    # the manifest that describes them exactly as they were.
    require_data_rows(outdir, sources)
    require_identity_columns(outdir, sources)
    rows_armed, member_armed = armed_guards(outdir, previous, sources)
    require_stable_baseline(outdir, previous, sources)

    # Both members AND the manifest, staged together, and the MANIFEST IS LAST.
    # write_atomically stages all-or-nothing but RENAMES in list order, and a
    # rename that fails part way through cannot be undone (bin/pyfunctions.py):
    # the directory can be left MIXED. With the manifest last, that leaves new
    # members under the OLD manifest -- a record whose row counts and member
    # names still describe a real fetch, so the next run's guards stay armed and
    # its refusal is honest. Manifest first would leave a manifest describing
    # files that were never written. The order is load-bearing; do not sort this
    # list.
    provenance = build_manifest(sources, offline, fetched_release, fetched_at)
    files = [(outdir / source.output_name, source.member_body) for source in sources.values()] + [
        (
            outdir / MANIFEST_NAME,
            (json.dumps(provenance, indent=2) + "\n").encode("utf-8"),
        )
    ]
    # The write is its own wait: the real members are ~250 MB together, staged
    # and then renamed, so a run that said nothing here would look finished long
    # before it was.
    log_info(
        f"Writing [{len(files)}] files ([{format_bytes(sum(len(body) for _, body in files))}]) "
        f"to [{outdir}]"
    )
    write_atomically(files, SCRIPT_NAME)

    print_summary(outdir, sources, offline, fetched_release, rows_armed, member_armed)
    print_load_command(outdir, sources)
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        sys.exit(main(sys.argv))
    except OSError as error:
        # The one boundary for a filesystem fault this script did not anticipate.
        # A traceback would print off the [FATAL] channel that bin/scripts-tests
        # and every operator grep key on, and would name a /nix/store path instead
        # of the thing that failed.
        fatal(f"[{type(error).__name__}] [{error}]")
